function horneyTriadenPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("horney-triaden")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Diagnostics</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">Horney Triads and Subtypes</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">This overview combines two highly compatible levels of personality diagnostics: the Horney triads as fundamental directions of human movement toward the world, and the instinctual subtypes as life-fields in which this movement becomes concretely visible. Together, they form a nuanced, observable map that is extremely helpful in profiling.</p>

      <img src="../assets/horney-triaden-subtypen.jpg" alt="Horney Triads and Subtypes"
        style="width:100%;border-radius:12px;margin:0 auto 2.5rem;display:block;" />

      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:1rem;color:var(--ink);">1. The Horney Triads: Directions of Movement Toward the World</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Karen Horney described three fundamental ways in which people relate to the world under inner tension. These directions of movement are not conscious decisions, but deeply imprinted orientations that form early and shape the entire pattern of relating.</p>

        <div class="vb-typ">
          <h3 class="vb-typ__head"><span class="vb-typ__nr">Toward the World</span> &ndash; Adaptation (Types 1, 2, 6)</h3>
          <p class="vb-typ__text">People in this triad seek security, orientation, and belonging through adaptation. They move toward the world, responding to expectations, rules, or the needs of others, and try to establish stability through correct, helpful, or loyal behavior. Attention is strongly directed outward &ndash; toward what is required or expected.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="vb-typ">
          <h3 class="vb-typ__head"><span class="vb-typ__nr">Against the World</span> &ndash; Assertion (Types 3, 7, 8)</h3>
          <p class="vb-typ__text">Here, an expansive movement is evident: the world is not primarily answered, but shaped, influenced, or dominated. People in this triad act with energy, goal-orientation, and assertiveness. They seek impact, scope of action, and control. Internal tension is usually high, but is actively carried outward &ndash; activity generates relief.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="vb-typ">
          <h3 class="vb-typ__head"><span class="vb-typ__nr">Away from the World</span> &ndash; Withdrawal (Types 4, 5, 9)</h3>
          <p class="vb-typ__text">This direction of movement is characterized by distancing. People in this triad regulate inner tension by withdrawing &ndash; internally, emotionally, or physically. The world is observed, not immediately answered. What often appears calm on the outside conceals complex internal processes.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="vb-anmerkung"><em>These three directions of movement are not Enneagram centers (Gut, Heart, Head), but a relational perspective: How do I move toward the world when tension arises?</em></p>

        <div style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding-top:1.6rem;margin-top:1.5rem;">
          <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:1rem;color:var(--ink);">2. The Subtypes: The Field of Movement</h2>
          <p class="vb-intro">The subtypes describe not <em>how</em> someone moves, but <em>where</em> this movement primarily takes place.</p>
          <div style="display:grid;gap:.5rem;font-size:.85rem;margin:.8rem 0 .4rem;line-height:1.65;">
            <div><span style="font-weight:700;color:var(--copper);">Self-Preservation (SP)</span> &ndash; Focus on security, body, resources, and stability. The movement shows itself in questions of provision, protection, and resilience.</div>
            <div><span style="font-weight:700;color:var(--copper);">Social (SO)</span> &ndash; In the foreground are roles, belonging, responsibility, and position within the system. The movement unfolds in the context of groups and structures.</div>
            <div><span style="font-weight:700;color:var(--copper);">Sexual (SX)</span> &ndash; Oriented toward intensity, bonding, and resonance. The movement is strongly polarized, emotionally charged, and relationship-centered.</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding-top:1.6rem;margin-top:1.5rem;">
          <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:1rem;color:var(--ink);">3. Observable Markers in Profiling</h2>
          <p class="vb-intro">The overview deliberately highlights observable markers &ndash; not internal motives or biographical explanations. This is crucial for profiling work.</p>
          <div style="display:grid;gap:.5rem;font-size:.85rem;margin:.8rem 0;line-height:1.65;padding:.8rem 1rem;background:var(--paper);border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--line);">
            <div><span style="font-weight:700;">Gaze Quality:</span> Where do attention and energy spontaneously go? Searching, checking, focused, turned inward, fixating, or ambivalent.</div>
            <div><span style="font-weight:700;">Contact Style:</span> How is relationship initiated, held, or avoided? Contact-initiating, role-oriented, goal-directed, controlling, distanced, or shifting.</div>
            <div><span style="font-weight:700;">Tension Regulation:</span> How is inner tension processed? Held, controlled, expansive, reduced, dampened, or fluctuating.</div>
          </div>
          <p class="vb-intro">These markers are directly perceptible, repeatable, and independent of self-reports. Typology does not happen via stories &ndash; but via presence.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding-top:1.6rem;margin-top:1.5rem;">
          <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:1rem;color:var(--ink);">4. Interplay of Triad and Subtype in Contact</h2>
          <p class="vb-intro">Only the interplay of both levels produces diagnostic sharpness. A Type 6 in self-preservation shows adaptation completely differently than a Type 6 in the sexual subtype. The triad describes the direction of movement, the subtype describes the field, and the markers make it visible.</p>

          ${[
            {
              titel:"Toward the World &ndash; Adaptation (Types 1, 2, 6)",
              kontext:"In a group, someone presents a new task. People of this triad immediately check internally: What is expected here? What is right? What is needed? The gaze is searching or checking, attention directed outward.",
              beispiele:[
                ["SP","Does not speak up immediately, but watches attentively, nods, asks clarifying questions. Contact is established cautiously, tension is held.","Before I say something, I want to be sure it fits."],
                ["SO","Quickly visible role awareness. Taking on responsibility, structuring, reminding of rules. Tension is controlled.","I look at how we can manage this well together."],
                ["SX","The gaze seeks bonding. Contact is personal, affection-oriented, sometimes intense. Tension is internally high, emotionally perceptible.","I want to be seen &ndash; and to see you."],
              ]
            },
            {
              titel:"Against the World &ndash; Assertion (Types 3, 7, 8)",
              kontext:"In a discussion, uncertainty arises. While others are still considering, people of this triad take the floor, make a point, or actively steer the conversation.",
              beispiele:[
                ["SP","The gaze is focused, contact goal-directed. Businesslike, efficient. Tension is stably high, but controlled.","Let's decide and move on."],
                ["SO","Leadership is exercised. The gaze is commanding, contact directing. The person visibly takes charge of direction for the group.","This is how we'll do it &ndash; it's time now."],
                ["SX","The gaze can appear fixating, contact demanding. Tension very high, played out directly in the relationship.","I want a reaction, now."],
              ]
            },
            {
              titel:"Away from the World &ndash; Withdrawal (Types 4, 5, 9)",
              kontext:"In an intense conversation, someone leans back, becomes quieter, looks away or inward. Their participation appears reduced, but is internally active.",
              beispiele:[
                ["SP","The gaze turns inward, contact is minimal. Tension is reduced, the body withdraws.","I need space to sort this out for myself."],
                ["SO","Observes the group, participates selectively. Contact distanced, tension dampened.","I see what's happening here, without getting involved."],
                ["SX","The gaze is ambivalent, contact shifting. Closeness and withdrawal alternate, tension fluctuates.","I'm present &ndash; and then again, not."],
              ]
            },
          ].map(t => `
            <div class="vb-typ" style="margin-bottom:1.4rem;">
              <h3 class="vb-typ__head"><span class="vb-typ__nr" style="font-size:.75rem;">${t.titel.split(" &ndash; ")[0]}</span> ${t.titel.split(" &ndash; ").slice(1).join(" &ndash; ")}</h3>
              <p style="font-size:.83rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;margin:.4rem 0 .8rem;font-style:italic;">${t.kontext}</p>
              ${t.beispiele.map(([sub,obs,zitat]) => `
                <div style="margin-bottom:.7rem;padding:.6rem .8rem;background:var(--paper);border-radius:6px;border-left:2px solid var(--copper);">
                  <div style="font-size:.78rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--copper);margin-bottom:.25rem;">${sub}</div>
                  <div style="font-size:.82rem;line-height:1.6;margin-bottom:.25rem;">${obs}</div>
                  <div style="font-size:.8rem;font-style:italic;color:var(--muted);">„${zitat}"</div>
                </div>
              `).join("")}
            </div>
          `).join("")}
        </div>

        <div style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding-top:1.6rem;margin-top:1rem;">
          <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:1rem;color:var(--ink);">5. Significance for Teaching and Practice</h2>
          <p class="vb-intro">This overview is not a shortcut for typing, but an orientation guide for precise perception. It helps avoid premature interpretations, reduce confusion between types, and differentiate more finely between similar appearances.</p>
          <p class="vb-intro">It enables differences not only to be explained cognitively, but to be experienced phenomenologically: through observation, comparison, and the training of perception. Anyone who begins to pay attention to gaze, contact, and tension regulation often recognizes the patterns within a few minutes &ndash; even before any content is spoken.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="border-left:3px solid var(--gold);padding:.9rem 1.1rem;margin:1.8rem 0;background:var(--paper);border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;">
          <p style="margin:0;font-size:.92rem;color:var(--ink);line-height:1.8;font-style:italic;">Two people can say the exact same thing &ndash; yet look, enter into contact, and regulate tension completely differently. Exactly these differences are crucial in Enneagram Profiling. The graphic provides the map, and the practical examples bring it to life.</p>
        </div>

      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"enneagramm-profiling", label:"Enneagram Profiling"},
        {route:"subtypen-schaubilder", label:"Subtype Charts"},
        {route:"bedeutung-27-subtypen", label:"Meaning of the 27 Subtypes"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function illusionenPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("illusionen")}
    <section class="narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Depth Psychology</p>
      <h1>Illusions of the 9 Enneagram Types</h1>
      <p class="lead-small">Every Enneagram type lives according to a characteristic illusion &mdash; an unconscious delusion that governs life. What the type experiences as desirable is in reality a private reality that keeps them trapped.</p>
      <div class="psycho-img-wrap" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
        <img src="../assets/schaubilder/illusionen/illusionen.jpg"
             alt="The delusional illusions of the 9 Enneagram types"
             class="psycho-img" />
      </div>
      <div style="max-width:640px;margin:0 auto 1.5rem;font-size:.92rem;line-height:1.8;color:var(--ink);">
        <p>Each type is caught in its own specific delusion, and each delusion carries the same hidden message &ndash; &bdquo;X is an illusion!&ldquo;: Type 9 lives the delusion of harmony, Type 1 the delusion of perfection, Type 2 the delusion of love, Type 3 the delusion of success, Type 4 the delusion of deficiency, Type 5 the delusion of scarcity, Type 6 the delusion of security, Type 7 the delusion of fun, and Type 8 the delusion of justice. What each type chases as an absolute truth is, at its core, exactly the illusion the chart names.</p>
      </div>
      ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "Seeing through the type&rsquo;s core delusion &ndash; and finding the essence that lies beneath it.", "Wer du wirklich bist")}
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"wer-wir-sind", label:"Who We Are"},
        {route:"ego-fixierungen", label:"Ego Fixations"},
        {route:"schutzdefizite", label:"Protection Deficits"},
      ])}
    </section>
  `);
}

function schutzdefizitePage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("schutzdefizite")}
    <section class="narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Triads</p>
      <h1>Schutz-Defizite der Triaden</h1>
      <p class="lead-small">The gut, heart, and head triads each carry a characteristic protection deficit: the gut triad fears restriction, the heart triad loss of love, the head triad loss of trust. These unconscious deficits shape the behavior of all three types within a triad.</p>
      <div class="psycho-img-wrap" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
        <img src="../assets/schaubilder/schutzdefizite/schutzdefizite.jpg"
             alt="Die Schutz-Defizite der Bauch-, Herz- und Kopftriade"
             class="psycho-img" />
      </div>
      <div style="margin:2.5rem 0 0;">
        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.4rem;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0 0 0.75rem;">Type Is Protection, Not Identity</h2>
        <img
          src="../assets/schutz.jpg"
          alt="Type is protection, not identity. Patterns arise where freedom was once wounded."
          style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;margin:0.75rem 0 1.25rem;"
        />
        <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:1rem 0;">This sentence strikes at the core of what protection deficits actually are. An Enneagram type is not a character trait that you have like hair color. It is a reaction &ndash; an early-developed strategy to deal with a world that did not feel safe at a decisive point.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:1rem 0;">The gut triad developed patterns around restriction: Where autonomy was once called into question, the need arose to assert, control, or withdraw oneself. The heart triad developed patterns around affection: Where love seemed conditional, the need arose to adapt, perform, or stand out. The head triad developed patterns around security: Where trust was once shaken, the need arose to analyze, anticipate, or hedge.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:1rem 0;">These patterns are not mistakes. They were clever solutions to real challenges. The problem arises when they continue to be automatically activated in adult life &ndash; even where the original threat has long ceased to exist. Recognizing your type therefore does not mean labeling a box. It means making the protection visible &ndash; and taking the first step toward freedom.</p>
      </div>

      ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "The nine types in their depth &ndash; protective patterns, passions, and the path to essence.", "Wer du wirklich bist")}
      ${bookTip("der-code-deiner-persoenlichkeit", "How the Enneagram type develops &ndash; and what it reveals about our deepest imprints.", "Der Code der Persönlichkeit")}
      ${bookTip("leidenschaft-und-heilung", "27 Subtypes: Passions, protective strategies, and paths to healing from therapeutic practice.", "Leidenschaft und Heilung")}
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"triadendefizite", label:"Triad Deficits"},
        {route:"grundformel", label:"Core Formula"},
        {route:"illusionen", label:"Illusions"},
      ])}
    </section>
  `);
}

function lasterTugendenAffirmationenPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("laster-tugenden-affirmationen")}
    <section class="narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Vices &middot; Virtues &middot; Affirmations</p>
      <h1>Laster, Tugenden & Affirmationen der 9 Enneagrammtypen</h1>
      <p class="lead-small">Every Enneagram type has a characteristic vice (the concentrated passion) and a virtue as its healing counterpart. The affirmations speak directly to the deepest need of each type.</p>
      <div class="psycho-img-wrap" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
        <img src="../assets/schaubilder/laster-tugenden-affirmationen/laster-tugenden-affirmationen.jpg"
             alt="Vices, Virtues, and Affirmations of the 9 Enneagram Types"
             class="psycho-img" />
      </div>
      <h2 style="margin-top:2.5rem; font-size:1.2rem; text-align:center; letter-spacing:0.05em; color:var(--ink);">Affirmations for the 9 Enneatypes</h2>
      <div style="margin-top:1rem; display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr 1fr; gap:0.75rem; grid-auto-rows:1fr;">
        <div style="background:#c49090; border-radius:0.5rem; padding:0.9rem 0.75rem; text-align:center; font-weight:500; font-size:0.9rem; color:#3a1010; display:flex; flex-direction:column; align-items:center; justify-content:center; min-height:110px;">Type 1<br><span style="font-weight:400; font-size:0.85rem;">You are a truly good person!</span></div>
        <div style="background:#7a2d90; border-radius:0.5rem; padding:0.9rem 0.75rem; text-align:center; font-weight:500; font-size:0.9rem; color:#fff; display:flex; flex-direction:column; align-items:center; justify-content:center; min-height:110px;">Type 2<br><span style="font-weight:400; font-size:0.85rem;">I'm so grateful for your help!</span></div>
        <div style="background:#9ee0d0; border-radius:0.5rem; padding:0.9rem 0.75rem; text-align:center; font-weight:500; font-size:0.9rem; color:#0a3028; display:flex; flex-direction:column; align-items:center; justify-content:center; min-height:110px;">Type 3<br><span style="font-weight:400; font-size:0.85rem;">You're doing a really great job!</span></div>
        <div style="background:#70e030; border-radius:0.5rem; padding:0.9rem 0.75rem; text-align:center; font-weight:500; font-size:0.9rem; color:#1a3a00; display:flex; flex-direction:column; align-items:center; justify-content:center; min-height:110px;">Type 4<br><span style="font-weight:400; font-size:0.85rem;">I truly value you and your authentic nature!</span></div>
        <div style="background:#e09060; border-radius:0.5rem; padding:0.9rem 0.75rem; text-align:center; font-weight:500; font-size:0.9rem; color:#3a1800; display:flex; flex-direction:column; align-items:center; justify-content:center; min-height:110px;">Type 5<br><span style="font-weight:400; font-size:0.85rem;">Your wishes and needs are truly no burden!</span></div>
        <div style="background:#f5c0d0; border-radius:0.5rem; padding:0.9rem 0.75rem; text-align:center; font-weight:500; font-size:0.9rem; color:#5a1a28; display:flex; flex-direction:column; align-items:center; justify-content:center; min-height:110px;">Type 6<br><span style="font-weight:400; font-size:0.85rem;">God surely has wonderful plans for you!</span></div>
        <div style="background:#f8d820; border-radius:0.5rem; padding:0.9rem 0.75rem; text-align:center; font-weight:500; font-size:0.9rem; color:#3a2e00; display:flex; flex-direction:column; align-items:center; justify-content:center; min-height:110px;">Type 7<br><span style="font-weight:400; font-size:0.85rem;">Your happiness in life truly matters to me!</span></div>
        <div style="background:#8b2014; border-radius:0.5rem; padding:0.9rem 0.75rem; text-align:center; font-weight:500; font-size:0.9rem; color:#fff; display:flex; flex-direction:column; align-items:center; justify-content:center; min-height:110px;">Type 8<br><span style="font-weight:400; font-size:0.85rem;">In your presence I always feel so safe, thank you!</span></div>
        <div style="background:#d0dc28; border-radius:0.5rem; padding:0.9rem 0.75rem; text-align:center; font-weight:500; font-size:0.9rem; color:#303800; display:flex; flex-direction:column; align-items:center; justify-content:center; min-height:110px;">Type 9<br><span style="font-weight:400; font-size:0.85rem;">Time with you is always very precious to me, thank you!</span></div>
      </div>
      <div style="margin:2.5rem 0 0;">
        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.4rem;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0 0 0.75rem;">The Virtue Is Already There</h2>
        <img
          src="../assets/tugend.jpg"
          alt="The type's virtue is already there – it is just covered by fear."
          style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;margin:0.75rem 0 1.25rem;"
        />
        <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:1rem 0;">This sentence changes the way one looks at vices and virtues. Often, the virtue is understood as a goal &ndash; as something one must first achieve once the vice has been overcome. Yet the Enneagram interprets it differently: The virtue is not the opposite of the vice. It is what lies beneath it.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:1rem 0;">Type 1 carries serenity within itself &ndash; concealed by the compulsion to do everything right. Type 4 carries equanimity within itself &ndash; concealed by the longing to be special. Type 8 carries innocence within itself &ndash; concealed by the fear of appearing vulnerable. The pattern is the same for all nine types: The virtue is not an achievement. It is what remains when fear subsides.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:1rem 0;">This is one of the deepest statements of the Enneagram: Development does not mean creating a new human being. It means uncovering what is already there &ndash; and what the ego has previously hidden with its protective strategies.</p>
      </div>

      ${bookTip("leidenschaft-und-heilung", "27 Subtypes &ndash; passions, virtues, and paths of healing directly from therapeutic practice.", "Leidenschaft und Heilung")}
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"heilungsweg", label:"Path of Healing"},
        {route:"leidenschaft-und-wunde", label:"Passion & Wound"},
        {route:"spirituelle-uebungen", label:"Spiritual Exercises"},
      ])}
    </section>
  `);
}

